      "The speech's famous sentence is usually quoted as a gesture of goodwill and is doing something more specific. In saying that we are all republicans and all federalists, the words name principles rather than parties, and the claim is that the disagreement was never about the form of the government.",
      "Whether that claim was true is a separate question from what it accomplished. A speech describing a division as shallower than it is can help make it shallower, and an incoming administration asserting that its opponents accept the constitution makes the opposite assertion harder to make.",
      "The passage on dissent goes considerably further than goodwill. It says that those who would dissolve the union or change its form should be left undisturbed, as monuments to the safety with which an error of opinion may be tolerated where reason is left free to combat it.",
